Periodic jobs, one per line, read by /etc/init.d/18_timers.sh at boot.

    NAME  TIMESPEC...  --  COMMAND...

NAME names the supervised service, so `service status timers` and
`ksvc check kdos-timer-NAME` can both find it. TIMESPEC is `snooze`'s own
options and nothing else is invented here. `--` ends the timespec: without it
nothing can tell where snooze's options stop and the command begins, because
both are bare words.

THE COMMAND IS AN ARGUMENT VECTOR AND NOT A SHELL LINE. No pipe, no
redirection, no glob, no `&&` — a program that needs to write a file takes a
flag that names it. That is the same rule every other configuration file in
this tree keeps, and it is why this one can be parsed rather than sourced.

A LINE THAT DOES NOT PARSE IS REPORTED AND SKIPPED, never guessed at: a timer
that silently did not start is indistinguishable from one that has not fired
yet, and the difference can be months.

The per-user tables have the same shape and the same parser
(/usr/local/lib/kdos/timers.sh): ~/.config/kdos/timers.d/ for each user's own
jobs, and /etc/kdos/user-services/timers.d/ for jobs every user runs as
themselves. Both run under that user's `timers` service
(/etc/kdos/user-services/30_timers.sh), from the user's first login to the
user's last, or for good once the user lingers.
